   ],
   "textLength": 381,
   "text": "Supporting your child’s connection with the world’s oldest continuing living culture is one of the greatest gifts you can offer them while raising them in Australia. But if you’re new to the country, you may be unsure where to begin. Here are some starting points and ideas to help you and your child learn about Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, cultures and history.",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 1,
    "listItems": 0,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 0,
    "repeats": 1,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
   "geometry": {
    "top": 1410,
    "height": 224,
    "widthFraction": 0.47,
    "leftFraction": 0.28,
    "pageFraction": 0.16,
    "aboveFold": false
   }
  },
  {
   "index": 11,
   "tag": "section",
   "class": "HighlightsBlock-module__EKX2iW__container",
   "landmarks": [
    "main",
    "article",
    "section"
   ],
   "textLength": 494,
   "text": "Key Points\nLearning about Australia’s First Nations is a process involving both child and parent \nThere is a range of age-appropriate resources online to support your child’s engagement with Indigenous Australia \nAttending events, travelling mindfully and doing your own research are ways to connect with First Nations people in your area and beyond \nMaking mistakes is part of the learning process, ",
   "counts": {
    "links": 0,
    "images": 0,
    "headings": 1,
    "listItems": 4,
    "tableRows": 0,
    "controls": 0,
    "paragraphs": 0,
    "repeats": 1,
    "coverLinks": 0
   },
   "linkTextRatio": 0,
